#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6456693-8845-5f7b-8b0b-2d6802bbf72e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Constant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79598fea-6ce9-502a-99b3-653e7b709cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79598fea-6ce9-502a-99b3-653e7b709cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573580} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1e5e139-150a-564f-8373-da7cbc3fc219" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9945bb2-9891-5ac6-bca2-13e5bf3bd964" :question/math-academy-id "q-302063" :question/problem "If $p(x)=3{x}^{6}$ and $q(x)=4x,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{{x}^{5}\\cdot q(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{5}\\cdot q(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{5}\\cdot q(x)={x}^{5}\\cdot (4x)=4{x}^{6}.\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions $p(x)$ and ${x}^{5}\\cdot q(x),$ we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{{x}^{5}\\cdot q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{6}}{4{x}^{6}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573868 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6456693-8845-5f7b-8b0b-2d6802bbf72e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Constant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05a2d4be-99ba-5100-b948-fcda87db3e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05a2d4be-99ba-5100-b948-fcda87db3e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573429} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fb27858-d0c0-52ea-8a05-60fc113b0b6a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a3cac7e-5a2a-596f-b2a3-d5e28243a2e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-336962" :question/problem "If $r(x)=-2{x}^{8}$ and $s(x)=\\frac{11}{{x}^{10}}$, then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot r(x)\\cdot s(x))=$  \n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot r(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot r(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot (-2{x}^{8})=-2{x}^{10}\n$$\n\nWhen we multiply the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot r(x)$ and $s(x)$, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot r(x)\\cdot s(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-2{x}^{10}\\cdot \\frac{11}{{x}^{10}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-22 \\\\  & =-22\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105854 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bea806-2913-57f5-ba32-be7aeda5bd15" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20db05d5-2cd1-5bb5-a51c-7ab499331004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573460} {:answer/id #uuid "43b84476-20bb-5bf1-ae69-7df330df7758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573507} {:answer/id #uuid "9167ac6f-2404-5e43-98e0-937156b1b81c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573611} {:answer/id #uuid "94cff7bf-1bad-5286-a7d3-89bb7100fe1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573617} {:answer/id #uuid "c7eaf0c5-8dba-59d1-9c0f-0d3682cb0ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9167ac6f-2404-5e43-98e0-937156b1b81c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573611} :answer-field/id #uuid "38288899-0ba2-57a8-830a-6a41d6af74e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e570ac4-c7f5-5887-a19e-4808c72f9d67" :question/math-academy-id "q-99288" :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=2x$ and $q(x)=7{x}^{4},$ what is ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x))?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2x\\cdot 7{x}^{4}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }((2\\cdot 7){x}^{1+4}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(14\\cdot {x}^{5}) \\\\  & =14\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{5}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{5}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $14$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{5} &={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{5}=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(14{x}^{5})=\\infty .\n$$"}]}
